# `lti::StateSpace::outputs`

[Back to `lti::StateSpace`](../StateSpace.md)

The number of outputs, `p`.

## Signature

```cpp
std::int64_t lti::StateSpace::outputs() const;
```

## What It Does

Returns `p`, the number of outputs, read from the second-to-last dimension of `C`. It is the quickest way to size a vector or loop that accompanies the system without taking a matrix apart. The value is an integer count and does not depend on any batch axes.

## Returns

`p` as a non-negative integer.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

The oscillator reports one output, the position.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace lti = pluss::subsp::eng::lti;
using namespace pluss::tils;

static pluss::Series mat(std::vector<double> v, std::int64_t r, std::int64_t c) { return pluss::make_series(std::move(v), {r, c}); }
// A damped oscillator x'' + 0.4 x' + 4 x = u with the position as output.
static lti::StateSpace oscillator(double sample_time = 0.0) {
  return lti::state(mat({0.0, 1.0, -4.0, -0.4}, 2, 2), mat({0.0, 1.0}, 2, 1), mat({1.0, 0.0}, 1, 2), mat({0.0}, 1, 1), sample_time);
}

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  out.writeln("outputs =", oscillator().outputs());
}
```

### Example 2

Measuring both states gives two outputs.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace lti = pluss::subsp::eng::lti;
using namespace pluss::tils;

static pluss::Series mat(std::vector<double> v, std::int64_t r, std::int64_t c) { return pluss::make_series(std::move(v), {r, c}); }
// A damped oscillator x'' + 0.4 x' + 4 x = u with the position as output.
static lti::StateSpace oscillator(double sample_time = 0.0) {
  return lti::state(mat({0.0, 1.0, -4.0, -0.4}, 2, 2), mat({0.0, 1.0}, 2, 1), mat({1.0, 0.0}, 1, 2), mat({0.0}, 1, 1), sample_time);
}

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const lti::StateSpace s = lti::state(mat({0.0, 1.0, -4.0, -0.4}, 2, 2), mat({0.0, 1.0}, 2, 1), mat({1.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0}, 2, 2), mat({0.0, 0.0}, 2, 1));
  out.writeln("outputs =", s.outputs());
}
```

### Example 3

All three counts of one system.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace lti = pluss::subsp::eng::lti;
using namespace pluss::tils;

static pluss::Series mat(std::vector<double> v, std::int64_t r, std::int64_t c) { return pluss::make_series(std::move(v), {r, c}); }
// A damped oscillator x'' + 0.4 x' + 4 x = u with the position as output.
static lti::StateSpace oscillator(double sample_time = 0.0) {
  return lti::state(mat({0.0, 1.0, -4.0, -0.4}, 2, 2), mat({0.0, 1.0}, 2, 1), mat({1.0, 0.0}, 1, 2), mat({0.0}, 1, 1), sample_time);
}

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const lti::StateSpace s = oscillator();
  out.writeln("states", s.states(), " inputs", s.inputs(), " outputs", s.outputs());
}
```

## See Also

- [`states`](states.md): the number of states
- [`inputs`](inputs.md): the number of inputs
- [`c`](c.md): the matrix whose size it reads

